::ALG2_U06_L03_GP_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L03_GP_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FM.1<br><br>Question:<br>A scatterplot has points that rise at a nearly constant rate. Which model type is most reasonable? {
= linear#Correct. A nearly constant rate of change is modeled well by a linear function.
~ quadratic#Quadratic data usually curves and has changing first differences.
~ exponential#Exponential data changes by a repeated factor, not a constant amount.
~ no model#A clear increasing pattern supports trying a model.
}

::ALG2_U06_L03_GP_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L03_GP_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FM.1<br><br>Question:<br>A model predicts 38, and the actual value is 42. What is the residual? {
~ -4#This subtracts actual from predicted instead of actual minus predicted.
= 4#Correct. Residual = actual - predicted = 42 - 38 = 4.
~ 38#This is the predicted value, not the prediction error.
~ 80#This adds actual and predicted instead of finding the difference.
}

::ALG2_U06_L03_GP_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L03_GP_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FM.1<br><br>Question:<br>Using a model to predict inside the observed data range is called what? {
~ extrapolation#Extrapolation predicts outside the observed data range.
~ residual analysis#Residual analysis checks prediction errors.
= interpolation#Correct. Interpolation makes a prediction within the observed data range.
~ correlation#Correlation describes strength and direction, not prediction location.
}

::ALG2_U06_L03_GP_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L03_GP_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FM.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which evidence best supports choosing an exponential model? {
~ The data has a constant difference.#A constant difference supports a linear model.
~ The residuals form a strong curve.#Curved residuals suggest the chosen model may not fit.
~ The data has no visible trend.#No visible trend does not support an exponential model.
= The data changes by a nearly constant ratio.#Correct. A repeated ratio is a clue for exponential behavior.
}

::ALG2_U06_L03_GP_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L03_GP_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FM.1<br><br>Question:<br>For y=6x+14, what is the predicted value when x=5? {
= 44#Correct. Substitute x=5 to get y=6(5)+14=44.
~ 30#This multiplies 6 and 5 but omits the intercept.
~ 19#This adds x and 14 instead of using the model.
~ 84#This multiplies 14 by 6 instead of substituting x.
}
